And so, now we have this kind of database of questions and answers.

00:42:42.269 --> 00:42:46.628
And of course, people ask the same question, but there's no great way to say, here's all

00:42:46.628 --> 00:42:50.068
the similar questions that you asked in the past.

00:42:50.068 --> 00:42:54.308
And so, I was like, okay, well, I can at least do some related.

00:42:54.308 --> 00:42:56.428
When I look at one question, show me the related question.

00:42:56.428 --> 00:42:58.509
So, I was like, well, that's easy to do now.

00:42:58.509 --> 00:43:03.909
Just do some embeddings and then find the ones that are closest.

00:43:03.909 --> 00:43:09.068
But that's making me think it would be great to have a map of this as well, so you can

00:43:09.068 --> 00:43:16.428
kind of see these are the sort of clusters that questions tend to come into.

00:43:16.428 --> 00:43:23.909
And that's exactly your example of, here's an unstructured data analysis, where we 100%

00:43:23.909 --> 00:43:29.868
know that there's some really rich structure here, but how do you quickly figure that out

00:43:29.868 --> 00:43:34.108
and iterate and then give it to people in a way that they can understand?

00:43:34.108 --> 00:43:35.108
Yeah.

00:43:35.108 --> 00:43:36.108
Yeah.

00:43:36.108 --> 00:43:38.988
It sounds like it's probably a fascinating dataset.

00:43:38.988 --> 00:43:43.988
There are probably some really tight clusters where people keep asking almost the same question.

00:43:43.988 --> 00:43:47.709
And then there's probably some interesting clusters where it's diffuse and it's lots

00:43:47.709 --> 00:43:53.149
of people asking variations of the same question, but heading in all sorts of different directions.

00:43:53.149 --> 00:43:58.248
I mean, there's so much you could learn and explore in that sort of data.

00:43:58.248 --> 00:44:06.068
And so, yeah, this is the tooling that I want to try and build is how to explore when it's

00:44:06.068 --> 00:44:09.188
not a nice tabular dataset anymore.

00:44:09.188 --> 00:44:10.188
Yeah.

00:44:10.188 --> 00:44:17.789
And also accepting that any 2D rendering is going to be a massive simplification of reality.

00:44:17.789 --> 00:44:23.548
And you probably don't just want one, but you want a selection that maybe people can

00:44:23.548 --> 00:44:24.889
iterate through.

00:44:24.889 --> 00:44:27.709
But then how do you help them find the right one for their needs?

00:44:27.709 --> 00:44:30.748
And yeah, lots of interesting questions.

00:44:30.748 --> 00:44:31.748
Yeah.

00:44:31.909 --> 00:44:39.709
I mean, my mental model of these things, especially the 2D map views, is that it's a lens through

00:44:39.709 --> 00:44:43.748
which you're viewing the data and it's deliberately providing a distortion.

00:44:43.748 --> 00:44:48.628
I mean, a lens is not very useful unless you want a microscope lens that will show you

00:44:48.628 --> 00:44:54.868
detail or a telescope lens that'll take in a lot of information and condense it down.

00:44:54.868 --> 00:44:56.548
Whatever you do, you're going to distort the data.

00:44:56.548 --> 00:45:00.909
So you just want to have as many different lenses through which you can look at the data.

00:45:00.909 --> 00:45:05.188
And so you just want to swap between lenses so you get these different views.

00:45:05.188 --> 00:45:09.668
And that's, I think, the best way you can get to data understanding.

00:45:09.668 --> 00:45:15.509
Because, yeah, I mean, to bring it back to maps again, the classic example of this is

00:45:15.509 --> 00:45:21.949
the London tube map, which fundamentally distorts distance but gives you a much better understanding

00:45:21.949 --> 00:45:24.029
of the network.

00:45:24.029 --> 00:45:27.988
You don't want multiple views like that of your data.

00:45:27.988 --> 00:45:28.988
Yeah.

00:45:28.988 --> 00:45:29.988
Yeah.

00:45:30.068 --> 00:45:35.668
And if you've ever looked at the London tube map rendered onto an actual geographic map,

00:45:35.668 --> 00:45:38.428
it's a mess and hard to make any sense of.

00:45:38.428 --> 00:45:42.108
But at least you understand how far apart some of the stations are, which is not at

00:45:42.108 --> 00:45:45.349
all obvious from the classic map.

00:45:45.349 --> 00:45:47.349
And these are the challenges.

00:45:47.349 --> 00:45:51.428
You want to present different views for different tasks.

00:45:51.428 --> 00:45:59.188
I did cognitive psychology and a little neuroscience, and that does remind me of brain anatomy,

00:46:00.068 --> 00:46:04.149
where we have names for a lot of the different parts of the brain and how they connect to

00:46:04.149 --> 00:46:05.149
each other.

00:46:05.149 --> 00:46:10.868
But obviously, all our brains are different, even the shape of various places.

00:46:10.868 --> 00:46:17.428
So somehow we agreed upon these labels, but also in research, you have to figure out how

00:46:17.428 --> 00:46:25.628
to mash them onto each other, register it onto a canonical brain.

00:46:25.628 --> 00:46:32.628
And it is interesting that need to categorize the parts, but also sometimes map them onto

00:46:32.628 --> 00:46:38.188
each other and what you keep and what you lose in the process.

00:46:38.188 --> 00:46:39.188
Yeah.

00:46:39.188 --> 00:46:46.349
I mean, it's the constant conflict between the desire to understand it in the continuous

00:46:46.349 --> 00:46:53.589
space and our constant desire to be able to categorize.

00:46:53.589 --> 00:47:00.149
These are always in conflict, but they're both useful and you've got to be able to somehow

00:47:00.149 --> 00:47:04.668
pivot between those two instincts that I think we have.

00:47:04.668 --> 00:47:10.389
That also reminds me of Procrustes analysis, which I kind of love because it's so called

00:47:10.389 --> 00:47:16.228
because this Greek myth of Procrustes, who is like a smith who used his hammer to make

00:47:16.228 --> 00:47:19.149
people fit the shape of his bed exactly.

00:47:19.149 --> 00:47:24.868
And that's kind of like what you're doing in this brain, you're just hammering bits

00:47:24.868 --> 00:47:30.149
to make it fit this reference brain.

00:47:30.149 --> 00:47:31.149
I was curious.

00:47:31.149 --> 00:47:38.029
Do you drive anyone crazy from your desire to categorize and dimensionality reduce?

00:47:38.029 --> 00:47:42.909
Is there anyone who's like, you got to stop clustering things?

00:47:42.909 --> 00:47:48.309
So my colleagues are all great and they're mostly the people I talk to.

00:47:48.309 --> 00:47:54.748
So I don't have that problem.

00:47:54.748 --> 00:47:59.509
One of the advantages of working at the Institute, as I said, I'm from the pure math side.

00:47:59.509 --> 00:48:03.108
There are the data science people who get more and more applied.

00:48:03.108 --> 00:48:09.429
And so generally speaking, they're the people talking to the frontline analysts actually

00:48:09.429 --> 00:48:10.549
doing the work.

00:48:10.549 --> 00:48:15.909
So I want that one level of remove where the problems get abstracted a little bit away

00:48:15.909 --> 00:48:19.988
to something I can work on.

00:48:19.988 --> 00:48:23.628
So I guess that saves me from having to be told that.

00:48:23.628 --> 00:48:30.549
I'm sure they are sick of being told that maps or clusters or summarizations like this

00:48:30.549 --> 00:48:33.228
are the answer.

00:48:33.228 --> 00:48:37.409
I mean, again, it's an answer.

00:48:37.409 --> 00:48:40.269
It's what I've got.

00:48:40.269 --> 00:48:41.269
It's the hammer.

00:48:41.269 --> 00:48:47.868
I'll throw at things, but I'm not going to pretend that it's the answer.

00:48:47.868 --> 00:48:52.409
It's just the best tools I have at hand at the moment.

00:48:52.409 --> 00:48:57.069
If somebody can come up with some better tools, I'll be more than happy to start reaching

00:48:57.069 --> 00:48:59.909
out in that direction.

00:48:59.909 --> 00:49:05.269
Do you see those like tendencies playing out in your personal life as well?

00:49:05.868 --> 00:49:10.868
Are there like households or tasks that you want to map?

00:49:10.868 --> 00:49:13.868
No, actually.

00:49:13.868 --> 00:49:20.988
My work and personal life manages to stay reasonably separate.

00:49:20.988 --> 00:49:23.228
So I think that one's a good one.

00:49:23.228 --> 00:49:26.049
I'm glad it stays that way.

00:49:26.049 --> 00:49:29.948
Like household chores, you're not like, this is a high dimensional space.

00:49:29.948 --> 00:49:31.748
We got to reduce it.

00:49:31.748 --> 00:49:33.149
No, no, no.

00:49:33.149 --> 00:49:36.828
At some point, I just want to get things done.

00:49:36.828 --> 00:49:43.868
Or are you the person who wants things to be perfectly arranged in the dishwasher for

00:49:43.868 --> 00:49:46.269
maximum packing efficiency?

00:49:46.269 --> 00:49:47.269
Yeah.

00:49:47.269 --> 00:49:53.509
So, no, it turns out that's not me.

00:49:53.509 --> 00:49:58.468
I think that's a thing that people expect in some ways, maybe from a mathematician,

00:49:58.468 --> 00:50:03.748
is like the desire for organization.

00:50:03.748 --> 00:50:06.948
And I drive my wife crazy because she's a list maker.

00:50:06.948 --> 00:50:11.828
She always wants to make lists, to-do lists, any lists to check things off.

00:50:11.828 --> 00:50:18.509
And I don't, and she gets very frustrated with me because I'm not organized and making

00:50:18.509 --> 00:50:23.029
lists and keeping everything neatly categorized.

00:50:23.029 --> 00:50:27.669
She's probably the one more likely to rearrange things in the dishwasher than I am to do better

00:50:27.909 --> 00:50:33.748
So, yeah, oddly enough, I'm on the opposite of the stereotype, I guess.

00:50:34.468 --> 00:50:42.389
I saw that you mentioned like imposter syndrome that even, I mean, I would say you like have

00:50:42.389 --> 00:50:48.868
an incredible list of accomplishments and I would say like dancing in pure mathematics

00:50:48.868 --> 00:50:52.549
and in data science is a pretty astounding feat.

00:50:52.789 --> 00:51:00.549
I was really surprised to see you mention that you experienced imposter syndrome in

00:51:00.549 --> 00:51:01.269
some of the places.

00:51:01.269 --> 00:51:03.269
I think that's really common.

00:51:04.468 --> 00:51:09.189
But I'm curious if you could say a bit about that piece.

00:51:10.868 --> 00:51:11.429
Sure.

00:51:11.429 --> 00:51:17.909
Well, I mean, one example you said is I'm in pure math and data science, but that mostly

00:51:17.909 --> 00:51:22.468
means I feel like I'm failing to keep up with the pure math.

00:51:22.468 --> 00:51:29.828
Like I go to math conferences and it's a struggle because there are people doing amazing things

00:51:29.828 --> 00:51:32.228
that I can barely wrap my head around.

00:51:33.669 --> 00:51:40.789
And then in data science, I'm a dilettante, dabbling in data science.

00:51:40.789 --> 00:51:45.588
I'm not a data science expert and I don't really know what I'm doing.

00:51:45.588 --> 00:51:48.309
So I feel out of place in both worlds.

00:51:50.069 --> 00:51:57.269
I have ended up writing software tools in Python, but I'm certainly not a software engineer

00:51:57.269 --> 00:51:58.789
and I don't really feel like one.

00:52:00.149 --> 00:52:04.789
And I constantly feel like I don't know what I'm doing as far as software engineering goes.

00:52:05.669 --> 00:52:12.468
I'm doing the best I can and it seems to be working, but this is an ongoing thing.

00:52:12.708 --> 00:52:15.029
I constantly feel a little bit out of place.

00:52:15.029 --> 00:52:21.909
And so, yeah, imposter syndrome is certainly something that I do try to deal with.

00:52:22.708 --> 00:52:28.069
And so mostly my way to deal with that is to just try and keep my head down and focused on

00:52:29.269 --> 00:52:34.948
what I'm doing and try not to worry about how it fits into everything else.

00:52:34.948 --> 00:52:40.228
Which, as you mentioned, it happens to show up in lots of places.

00:52:40.228 --> 00:52:47.988
So I guess I get people reaching out and talking about it in these ways, but I try

00:52:47.988 --> 00:52:55.748
and keep my head down because otherwise I feel like I mostly don't know anything about anything

00:52:57.029 --> 00:52:58.948
and I'm just doing the best I can.

00:53:00.468 --> 00:53:05.029
Yeah, I'd imagine when you're straddling, it sounds like when you're straddling two

00:53:05.988 --> 00:53:12.389
areas, that's like a big challenge of just the net you've cast.

00:53:13.669 --> 00:53:14.628
But I don't know.

00:53:14.628 --> 00:53:20.228
It's so interesting to hear because I feel like it's such a useful spot to be in,

00:53:20.228 --> 00:53:24.549
like that kind of interdisciplinary zone.

00:53:25.269 --> 00:53:28.069
I'm a huge fan of interdisciplinary work.

00:53:28.069 --> 00:53:34.789
I think I really feel like that's where a lot of really useful things happen.

00:53:35.348 --> 00:53:42.149
When different things end up mixing together, that's when interesting things happen.

00:53:42.149 --> 00:53:47.269
And so I'm happy to be able to be in this interdisciplinary spot.

00:53:47.269 --> 00:53:51.269
As I said, I feel like that's one of the real strengths of the TUT Institute,

00:53:51.269 --> 00:53:55.669
the Research Institute, is that we have a bunch of this interdisciplinary.

00:53:55.669 --> 00:53:59.748
And I mean, to some people, I guess it's not very interdisciplinary because it's like math

00:53:59.748 --> 00:54:03.429
and computer science and data science, and maybe those are all the same as far as some

00:54:03.429 --> 00:54:04.228
people are concerned.

00:54:04.228 --> 00:54:08.868
But they're wildly different to people working in those fields.

00:54:08.868 --> 00:54:13.189
I would love to see more, more interdisciplinary work.

00:54:14.069 --> 00:54:19.748
I was curious too, like in the, this is a bit of a tangent, but I noticed like on the

00:54:19.748 --> 00:54:26.069
topic of sort of doing programming and pure math, I noticed, are you, you're working on

00:54:26.069 --> 00:54:28.069
a Rust library now too, is that?

00:54:28.069 --> 00:54:29.828
Who isn't working on a Rust library?

00:54:30.628 --> 00:54:41.669
So actually that one is, I said I needed projects so I could try out AI and learn how good it

00:54:41.669 --> 00:54:42.708
is at programming.

00:54:42.708 --> 00:54:46.069
So that was one of my AI projects.

00:54:46.948 --> 00:54:54.468
I am not Rust fluent at all, but I had a Python library for approximate nearest neighbor search

00:54:54.628 --> 00:55:02.389
that made use of the number library, which JIT compiles the Python down to LLVM machine

00:55:02.389 --> 00:55:02.948
code.

00:55:02.948 --> 00:55:06.309
So it actually runs surprisingly fast, even though it's pure Python.

00:55:06.868 --> 00:55:13.269
And so I was just curious, like this seemed like a good task for AI models.

00:55:13.988 --> 00:55:18.628
Rewrite this library in Rust, because you should be able to make it go faster.

00:55:19.348 --> 00:55:31.429
And so what I've just put up on GitHub is the final output, but it took way, way longer

00:55:31.429 --> 00:55:32.149
than I expected.

00:55:32.149 --> 00:55:36.708
I started on this well over a year ago.

00:55:37.988 --> 00:55:45.669
And the models back then, I think it was like Opus 4.5 with Claude I started out with.

00:55:46.628 --> 00:55:47.509
It could not do it.

00:55:49.588 --> 00:55:56.708
It wrote a Rust version of the library and it did function eventually, but there was

00:55:56.708 --> 00:56:03.269
a lot of back and forth with the model saying, this is great, but please run the benchmark

00:56:03.269 --> 00:56:05.108
again, because it's still slower than the Python.

00:56:06.868 --> 00:56:07.988
And it took a long time.

00:56:08.628 --> 00:56:13.029
And so I think only the latest models have actually gotten to a point where it could

00:56:13.029 --> 00:56:15.348
make it run faster than Python code.

00:56:15.988 --> 00:56:19.348
Well, because it was already being just in time compiled by number, right?

00:56:19.348 --> 00:56:19.828
Yeah.

00:56:19.828 --> 00:56:20.309
Yeah, yeah.

00:56:20.309 --> 00:56:29.108
It's fast Python code, but still, I feel like a Rust native implementation specialized

00:56:29.108 --> 00:56:31.988
should be able to get at least as performant as that.

00:56:33.429 --> 00:56:37.588
And so it turns out that this is a challenge for the model.

00:56:37.588 --> 00:56:41.429
So this was a learning experience for me to learn how good are the models at this.

00:56:41.429 --> 00:56:46.389
And so I think they've gotten there in as much as I've produced it, it's slightly

00:56:46.389 --> 00:56:48.868
faster than the Python in a number of ways.

00:56:48.868 --> 00:56:49.748
So this is good.

00:56:49.748 --> 00:56:53.348
It's not as flexible, but that's your trade-off.

00:56:54.468 --> 00:57:00.149
But that was me having a learning curve and an excuse to play around with the models.

00:57:00.149 --> 00:57:01.828
Yeah, it's such an interesting experiment.

00:57:03.269 --> 00:57:09.269
And I was curious as I saw it, the kind of like Rust library standing out to what's

00:57:09.269 --> 00:57:12.868
this point, it seems like a really nice testing ground to see.

00:57:13.669 --> 00:57:19.029
Do you, coming out of it, did you, do you feel like, did you learn anything useful for,

00:57:20.149 --> 00:57:24.708
like that you took out of it for working with AI, like coding with AI or?

00:57:25.509 --> 00:57:31.509
Yeah, I think the thing that really made it work was that there was a very targeted goal

00:57:31.509 --> 00:57:34.468
and I could actually have it write tests and benchmarks.

00:57:34.468 --> 00:57:35.988
I had test suites in the pipe.

00:57:35.988 --> 00:57:38.628
So there was, this was very much what Wes was talking about.

00:57:38.628 --> 00:57:44.228
If you can make it very specific, you can, you can have it run away and do a bunch of

00:57:44.228 --> 00:57:44.728
stuff.

00:57:45.108 --> 00:57:49.669
But it needed the specificity and it needed the, the ability to just go back and forth

00:57:49.669 --> 00:57:53.189
and benchmark against the Python code and have it run it itself and go, Oh yeah, no,

00:57:53.189 --> 00:57:53.828
you're right.

00:57:53.828 --> 00:57:54.789
It is still slower.

00:57:55.988 --> 00:58:00.309
That was kind of critical to manage for me to make it work.

00:58:00.309 --> 00:58:06.228
So I guess that that was one of the big takeaways for me is I, if I, if I want to actually have

00:58:06.228 --> 00:58:11.909
a large scale project, because this was, as opposed to just editing what's there a

00:58:11.909 --> 00:58:16.309
little bit, it needs to be able to be very specific.

00:58:16.309 --> 00:58:20.228
And I need to be able to frame everything that it needs to be able to do with a great

00:58:20.228 --> 00:58:21.669
deal of specificity.

00:58:21.669 --> 00:58:27.909
Do you have any new Rust libraries you'll be making or next experiments?

00:58:28.468 --> 00:58:30.228
Ah, I don't know.

00:58:30.228 --> 00:58:35.828
I mean, I guess in theory I should, I should see if it can port UMAP to Rust.

00:58:35.828 --> 00:58:37.828
I'm sure people have already done this.

00:58:37.828 --> 00:58:44.868
Um, but I mean, from the point of view of, of running the experiment to see what it can

00:58:44.868 --> 00:58:48.789
do, that would be, that would be a, uh, an interesting challenge.

00:58:48.789 --> 00:58:52.389
Um, I don't know how much difference that'll make.

00:58:53.108 --> 00:58:59.108
I, yeah, still, still learning Rust really myself that the LLMs are good at it though.

00:59:00.309 --> 00:59:04.309
I don't, you know, one last thing I wanted to bring up to make sure to bring up is, um,

00:59:05.108 --> 00:59:11.828
I, I heard in an interview with, um, Vincent Vormerdam that you did, he, he brought up

00:59:11.828 --> 00:59:18.948
a really interesting quote that I think Hadley gave at some point, um, which was essentially

00:59:18.948 --> 00:59:22.549
that algorithms can scale, but they often don't surprise you.

00:59:23.748 --> 00:59:25.828
Uh, does this sound familiar Hadley?

00:59:27.509 --> 00:59:32.389
Yeah, this was my, always my pitch for why you needed visualization and modeling like

00:59:32.948 --> 00:59:38.228
visualization doesn't scale because a human's got to look at it, but it can surprise you.

00:59:38.948 --> 00:59:44.389
And models like every model makes some assumptions and they can never question those

00:59:44.389 --> 00:59:44.868
assumptions.

00:59:44.868 --> 00:59:47.909
So at some level it can't surprise you.

00:59:49.269 --> 00:59:54.389
Uh, although now I have to maybe reconsider that if we think of LLMs as being a model,

00:59:54.389 --> 00:59:58.708
since they certainly like surprise us all the time in interesting ways.

00:59:58.708 --> 01:00:04.309
Different sense of model though, because I mean, it's, it's very much closer to a visualization

01:00:04.309 --> 01:00:09.108
and that it's distilling something out to a summary that you still have to feed through

01:00:09.108 --> 01:00:09.588
a human.

01:00:10.149 --> 01:00:15.029
So I'd put it in the same class as visualization.

01:00:15.029 --> 01:00:17.588
It's something you're feeding to, to a human.

01:00:17.588 --> 01:00:19.429
And that's the part that surprises you.

01:00:19.988 --> 01:00:20.948
I love that quote.

01:00:20.948 --> 01:00:22.309
I think it's fabulous.

01:00:22.309 --> 01:00:30.948
I reuse it all the time because it is very much, I think the motivation for the kind

01:00:30.948 --> 01:00:34.549
of exploratory work that, that I'm interested in.

01:00:34.549 --> 01:00:36.549
I want the thing that will surprise people.

01:00:38.309 --> 01:00:47.108
That's the most important part, um, because I, so I'm interested in helping people ask

01:00:47.108 --> 01:00:49.429
better questions rather than finding answers.

01:00:49.429 --> 01:00:52.948
Um, that's, I guess my.

01:00:48.902 --> 01:00:54.262
So, I'm interested in helping people ask better questions rather than finding answers.

01:00:55.462 --> 01:01:04.582
That's, I guess, my would-be quote. Because I think there are lots of ways to squeeze data

01:01:04.582 --> 01:01:11.781
and get answers. But I think knowing what question you should be asking is a much harder problem

01:01:11.781 --> 01:01:14.902
these days. Yeah, and I guess that goes back,

01:01:14.902 --> 01:01:21.942
too, to your focus on the EDA stage. Essentially, like, what question should I ask?

01:01:22.742 --> 01:01:29.622
Feeling like you're always at that early stage, trying to sort out the questions.

01:01:30.422 --> 01:01:34.021
Yeah. My colleague, John Healy, his go-to quote is just, look at your data.

01:01:35.222 --> 01:01:39.382
That's all he ever says. And he means that in the broadest possible sense of look at your data.

01:01:40.102 --> 01:01:45.861
Open it up in Excel and stare at lines in a spreadsheet. Whatever. Just actually look at

01:01:45.861 --> 01:01:54.022
what's in your data. I've seen him working with analysts who bring a data set. And he'll ask a

01:01:54.022 --> 01:01:58.501
bunch of questions about, well, what's true of this data? What do you at least know about it?

01:01:59.942 --> 01:02:04.582
Because whatever they say is true of the data, it's almost always a thing that he will then

01:02:04.582 --> 01:02:10.821
go and falsify by going through the data. No, that, in fact, is false. There are negative values

01:02:10.821 --> 01:02:19.062
in here. I'm sorry, but the timestamps are not contiguous and not they're duplicated in some

01:02:19.062 --> 01:02:25.302
places or whatever. Whatever assumptions people make about their data turn out not to be true.

01:02:25.302 --> 01:02:30.902
And so, that's a lot of the motivation for my desire to just try and ask better questions

01:02:31.622 --> 01:02:37.141
is people assume they know what's in data and it is almost never true. And you just need to

01:02:37.141 --> 01:02:41.702
be able to get people to a point where they can realize that for themselves.

01:02:42.262 --> 01:02:48.662
It seems like incredible advice. And especially in the AI era too, with evals, I guess that

